    "knowledge_point": "Simplifying Definite Integrals of Even Functions",
    "problem": "Simplify the integral ${\\int }_{-9}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x$ using symmetry.",
    "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *even* if $f(-x)=f(x)$\n\n- a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if $f(-x)=-f(x)$\n\nMoreover, for any even function $f(x),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ is symmetric across the $y$-axis, so we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-a}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{a}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x)={x}^{2}-3$ is an even function. Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =((-x)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =f(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, because $f(x)=({x}^{2}-3)$ is an even function, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-9}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x=2{\\int }_{0}^{9}({x}^{2}-3)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
